## Recovery and state-only destruction

Mutations and recovery use the shared client mutex; this does not lock out
out-of-band edits. GET reads honor configured retries. POST writes are attempted
once, redirects are rejected, and affirmative save plus authoritative requested-
field read-back is required before activation.

A confirmed or uncertain save retains fixed identity `ddns`, known state and
canonical private requested-field intent after a lost/malformed reply, read-back
failure, cancellation or activation failure. Preserve returned Terraform state
and refresh using a fresh plan. Recovery observes authoritative truth without
repeating set. Matching requested fields alone permit activation; omitted fields
do not veto recovery. Valid contradictions become authoritative state and clear
pending intent without activation; review a fresh plan. Malformed/unavailable
truth retains identity and intent, never infers singleton absence. Do not edit
private bytes. Field validation rejection is an error, not successful creation.

Terraform Core may taint failed creates. Recovery does not override its normal
replacement planning. Refresh and inspect the plan; recovery itself never repeats
a saved write.

**The provider's Delete operation is zero-API and state-only**, including
unconfigured/canceled clients and pending recovery. Delete never reads, resets,
stops or reconfigures Kea. However, normal Terraform Core planning refresh runs
Read before Delete: it may read/reconcile pending saves, activate verified matching
requested fields, or fail before Delete is reached. Therefore a whole
`terraform destroy` command is not guaranteed to make zero API calls or zero
activation calls. Disabling refresh is a distinct Core behavior, not a change to
Read recovery semantics.

If Delete receives pending recovery directly, it abandons that ownership and
activation intent without rolling back saved settings. Review appliance settings
and backup/state before reimport or manual reconciliation. Destroy is not a way
to disable DDNS.

Disabled-appliance lifecycle verification, implementation, merge and publication
are separate gates. Older-core/OpenTofu compatibility, daemon enablement and
actual DNS updates are not claimed by this work.

